WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:10.776
In Spain, 1,119 people died on interurban roads in 2025. Most of them on single-lane roads with sparse traffic and poor signal.

2
00:00:11.126 --> 00:00:21.323
SENDA rides with you: it warns of what you can't see, calls for help even with no network, and guides you if you stop to assist. It never replaces your attention or 112.

3
00:00:22.923 --> 00:00:33.483
Fog, 120 km/h, and 1.2 seconds between cars. Each driver reacts to the brake lights ahead and the delay adds up: five cars pile up.

4
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:45.567
With SENDA, the first phone detects hard braking from GPS speed and alerts everyone at once. Only the car tailgating right behind collides: only a safe following distance fixes that.

5
00:00:47.167 --> 00:00:56.340
A night crash along 18 kilometres with zero coverage. The phone in the wrecked car broadcasts an SOS, and any passing car with SENDA carries it out.

6
00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:04.306
With just one in ten cars, the alert gets out in about seven minutes. And oncoming cars warn drivers heading toward the crash.

7
00:01:05.906 --> 00:01:13.351
GPS works without the Internet. What's missing is knowing what to say: road code, kilometre marker, and direction of travel.

8
00:01:13.701 --> 00:01:21.061
SENDA computes it offline, adds the nearest visible landmark, such as a wind turbine, and tracks your position inside a tunnel.

9
00:01:22.661 --> 00:01:31.515
Anyone who stops can coordinate the scene following the official protocol: protect, alert, and assist. Every task is confirmed by a second person.

10
00:01:31.865 --> 00:01:39.694
When traffic police arrive, they take command and receive the summary. Confirmed actions earn civic points in a tamper-evident ledger.

11
00:01:41.294 --> 00:01:49.166
The first to know is the phone of the driver who took the wrong ramp: three consecutive positions against the carriageway flow are enough to warn them.

12
00:01:49.516 --> 00:01:55.020
Oncoming drivers receive the time until they cross paths and what to do: keep right and slow down.

13
00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:03.767
The tail of a traffic jam grows backward. Using traffic-flow theory, SENDA calculates how fast that shockwave moves.

14
00:02:04.117 --> 00:02:10.410
So it warns you where the tail of the queue will be when you reach it, not where it was, letting you slow down much earlier.

15
00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:18.538
SENDA only uses public traffic cameras released for reuse, and only to measure road conditions, never people.

16
00:02:18.888 --> 00:02:29.320
From a dark reference at a known distance, it calculates visibility in metres and the safe speed to stop within sight. This image is synthetic, built to verify the method.

17
00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:39.069
Both 112 and DATEX II traffic feeds are shared across the European Union. That is why the exact same core works in the Netherlands.

18
00:02:39.419 --> 00:02:46.523
There, a red cross above a lane means the lane is closed. SENDA reads it from open data and speaks the warning in Dutch.

19
00:02:48.123 --> 00:02:58.214
Speed cameras, message signs, and loop detectors aren't radios we can tap into: each talks to the traffic authority over a closed link. Radar detection is also illegal.

20
00:02:58.564 --> 00:03:06.863
Instead, SENDA fuses what the authority publishes. For example, loop-detector readings reveal where a queue starts and how fast it grows.

21
00:03:08.463 --> 00:03:14.927
Everything you have seen is computed by the same app core, backed by automated tests that run completely offline.

22
00:03:15.277 --> 00:03:22.914
The most important part is still ahead: real-road trials, the native app for car-to-car alerts, and agreements with traffic authorities.

23
00:03:24.514 --> 00:03:28.887
SENDA: the channel that remains when everything else fails. Try it on the web.
